    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most rooms the answer is simple: up to about 75 square feet, a 29 to 36 inch fan; up to about 144 square feet, 36 to 42 inches; up to about 225 square feet, 44 to 50 inches; up to about 400 square feet, 50 to 54 inches or a bit larger; and beyond that, 56 inches and up or more than one fan. Then hang it so the blades sit 8 to 9 feet off the floor, never lower than 7, and at least 18 inches from any wall. Blade span gets the fan into the right range. Ceiling height, mounting and CFM decide whether it actually works.
        </p>

        <div id="blade-span" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Blade Span by Room Size: The Sizing Chart
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade span is the diameter of the circle the blade tips sweep, measured tip to tip. On a fan with an even number of blades you can measure straight across; on a three- or five-blade fan, measure from the center of the motor to one tip and double it. That number, not the size printed on a marketing photo, is what you match to the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the floor area of the room: length times width in feet. The chart below combines the ENERGY STAR guidance published for U.S. homes with the manufacturer charts from brands like Hunter, which run a little larger for big rooms. Where the two disagree, the right-hand column is what we would actually buy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">ENERGY STAR guidance</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What we would buy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical rooms</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Up to 75 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">29 to 36 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 to 36 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Walk-in closet, laundry, small bath</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">76 to 144 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36 to 42 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">42 to 44 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small bedroom, home office, nursery</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">145 to 225 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">44 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">44 to 52 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Average bedroom, kitchen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">226 to 400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 to 54 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52 to 60 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Primary bedroom, living room</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not covered; use two fans</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 to 72 in, or two fans</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Great room, open plan</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            When a room falls between two sizes, go up rather than down. A larger fan running on medium moves more air, more quietly, than a smaller fan straining on high, and it spreads that air over a wider area. The exception is a small room where an oversized fan would end up closer than 18 inches to a wall, which is a hard limit covered further down.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 52-inch fan is the most common size sold for a reason: it suits the 150 to 350 square foot rooms that make up most of a typical house. If you are fitting out a standard bedroom or living room and want one number, 52 inches is it. Our pillar guide to the <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">best ceiling fans with lights</a> includes a 42-inch remote fan for small rooms, several 52 to 56 inch all-rounders, and a 70-inch fan rated for rooms up to 500 square feet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under 150 sq ft: 42 to 44 inches. 150 to 350: 52 inches. Over 400: 60-plus or two fans.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That covers the vast majority of rooms. Use the chart for the edge cases and size up when in doubt.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ceiling-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Downrod Length by Ceiling Height
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the blade span is settled, ceiling height decides how the fan hangs. Hunter, among others, recommends that blades sit about 8 to 9 feet above the floor. Too high and the airflow dissipates before it reaches anyone; too low and the fan is a hazard and a visual intrusion. The downrod, the metal pipe between the ceiling bracket and the motor, is how you put the blades at the right height.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hunter's published rule of thumb is to subtract 9 feet from the ceiling height to get the downrod length, since the fan body itself takes up roughly a foot. Its chart works out like this, and other major brands publish very similar figures. Always check your fan's manual, because a tall motor housing or a bowl light kit adds several inches.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ceiling height</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Downrod length</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8 ft or lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None (hugger or flush mount)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blades must still clear 7 ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">9 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard rod included with the fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually 3 to 6 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 to 18 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buy separately on most fans</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">11 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 to 24 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700"></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check wobble after install</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">13 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">48 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700"></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">14 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700"></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">15 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longest standard Hunter rod</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For sloped and vaulted ceilings, measure from the point where the fan will mount, not from the peak or the lowest wall. Most fans tolerate a modest slope on their standard ball-joint mount, but steep pitches need a sloped-ceiling adapter, and you must check that the blade tips clear the ceiling on the uphill side. A longer downrod is often the simplest way to buy that clearance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Longer downrods have a cost: they amplify any imbalance. A blade that is a few grams heavy produces a barely visible wobble on a 6-inch rod and a sway you can see from across the room on a 48-inch one. Balance the blades carefully on tall installs, and if a fan sways after hanging, our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-ceiling-fan-wobbling/">why a ceiling fan wobbles</a> covers the fixes from blade weights to bracket screws.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clearance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Clearances That Matter: 7 Feet, 18 Inches, and the Ceiling Gap
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three distances decide whether a fan is safe and whether it works. First, the blades must be at least 7 feet above the floor. Virtually every manufacturer's installation instructions specify this minimum, and it exists because a spinning blade at head height is a genuine injury risk. Lower than 7 feet is not a judgment call; if the ceiling cannot give you 7 feet under a hugger fan, the room should not have a ceiling fan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the blade tips should be at least 18 inches from any wall, sloped ceiling surface, beam or cabinet. Closer than that, the fan cannot draw air evenly around its edges, it becomes noisier, and the turbulence near the wall makes the airflow feel choppy. This is the number that most often limits fan size in small rooms. A 52-inch fan needs a room at least about 7.5 feet across in each direction for the tips to clear by 18 inches, and that is before you account for a door swinging open or a tall wardrobe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, the blades need space above them. Department of Energy-backed guidance from the Building America program recommends 8 to 10 inches between the blades and the ceiling, with 6 inches as an absolute minimum. A fan pulls air from above and pushes it down; when the blades are tight against the ceiling, they starve for air, and output drops. This is the real cost of a hugger fan, covered in the mounting section below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers to Measure</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">7 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum blade height off the floor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8-9 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ideal blade height</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum tip-to-wall distance</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8-10 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ideal blade-to-ceiling gap</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do not forget the box and the joist</h3>

          <p>
            Clearance is half of mounting; support is the other half. A ceiling fan must hang from an electrical box listed for fan support, typically stamped as acceptable for fan support with a weight rating, and secured to a joist or a fan-rated brace between joists. Under the National Electrical Code, a fan-rated box with no weight marking is good for fans up to 35 pounds; boxes marked for more can carry up to 70, and anything heavier must be supported independently of the box. If you are replacing a light fixture or picking a new location, a decent <a href="/best-stud-finder/">stud finder</a> will show you where the joists run so you know whether you can screw into one or need a brace bar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cfm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. CFM and CFM per Watt: The Numbers That Separate Good Fans From Bad
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade span tells you how big a fan is. CFM, cubic feet per minute, tells you how much air it moves, and that is what you actually feel. Two 52-inch fans can differ by thousands of CFM depending on blade pitch, blade shape and motor strength. A shallow-pitched decorative fan can look the part and barely stir a living room, while a well-designed fan of the same size produces a breeze you can feel across the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling fans sold in the U.S. carry an FTC EnergyGuide label that lists airflow in CFM, electricity use in watts and an estimated yearly energy cost, plus airflow efficiency in CFM per watt. Read it before you buy. As a practical benchmark, a mid-size bedroom or living room wants roughly 4,000 to 5,000 CFM on high, and a large or open-plan room wants 6,000 or more. Below about 3,000 CFM, a fan is fine for a small bedroom or office and underwhelming anywhere bigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Efficiency is the CFM per watt figure: how much air you get for each watt of electricity. Higher is better. ENERGY STAR certified fans are, on average, about 20 percent more efficient than standard models, and the current ENERGY STAR specification sets its bar as a formula tied to blade diameter, so bigger fans must hit higher efficiency numbers to qualify. If a fan you are considering is not certified, compare CFM per watt at medium speed, which is where most fans spend most of their time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Motor type drives most of the difference. Building America guidance notes that high-efficiency permanent-magnet motors, the DC motors in most premium fans, can be three to five times as efficient as standard fan motors, drawing 9 watts or less at medium speed against 30 to 40 watts for a conventional AC motor. You pay more up front, but for a bedroom fan that runs all night through the summer, the lower draw and the much quieter operation are worth it. Our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">ceiling fan buying guide</a> lists which picks use DC motors and how loud each one measured.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT TO COMPARE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">CFM on high for cooling power. CFM per watt at medium for running cost.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ignore blade count and blade length in isolation. A big fan with a weak motor and flat blades is the most common disappointment in this category.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="long-rooms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Long Rooms, Open Plans and When to Use Two Fans
          </h2>

          <p>
            Square footage alone misleads in long, narrow rooms. A 12-by-30 foot room is 360 square feet, which the chart says wants a 52 to 60 inch fan, but a single fan in the middle will leave both ends of the room nearly still. Airflow from a ceiling fan spreads in a circle, so it covers square-ish spaces well and rectangles poorly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to split the room into roughly square zones and size a fan for each. That 12-by-30 room becomes two 12-by-15 zones of 180 square feet each, and two 44 to 52 inch fans, one centered in each zone, will outperform any single large fan. A useful trigger: if the room is more than about one and a half times as long as it is wide, or larger than about 400 square feet, plan on two fans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Open-plan spaces work the same way. Rather than one huge fan over the middle of a combined kitchen and living area, put a fan over each place people actually sit or stand: the sofa, the dining table, the kitchen island. Center each fan on its zone rather than the room's geometry, and keep at least 18 inches between each fan's blade tips and any wall, and enough space between fans that their airflows do not collide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single big fan still has its place. In a large, roughly square great room with a tall ceiling, one 60 to 72 inch fan on a long downrod moves a lot of air with less visual clutter and one set of wiring. That is the case our pillar's large-room pick is designed for. Matching fan speeds also matters with multiple fans; DC fans with a shared remote or app make it easy to run two fans at the same setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the goal is to cool the whole house rather than the people in one room, no number of ceiling fans will do it, because they move air around without exchanging it. In climates with cool evenings, a <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/">whole-house fan</a> exhausts hot indoor air through the attic and pulls cooler air in through open windows, which genuinely drops the indoor temperature.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mounting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flush Mount vs Downrod: Which One Your Ceiling Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flush-mount fans, also called huggers or low-profile fans, mount the motor directly to the ceiling bracket with no downrod. They exist for one reason: ceilings around 8 feet or lower, where a downrod would put the blades below the 7-foot minimum. In a standard 8-foot room, a hugger typically leaves the blades at roughly 7 to 7.5 feet, which keeps the room usable for tall people.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is airflow. With the blades only a few inches from the ceiling, a hugger cannot draw air freely from above, so it moves less air than the same size fan on a short downrod. The ENERGY STAR specification recognizes this by setting a lower efficiency bar for hugger fans than for standard fans, which tells you the gap is built into the category, not a quirk of cheap models. A hugger is the right answer for a low ceiling, but do not choose one for a 9-foot room just for the look.
          </p>

          <p>
            On ceilings 9 feet and up, use a downrod. Even the short 3 to 6 inch rod that ships with most fans gets the blades into a better position for drawing air, and on taller ceilings a longer rod brings the blades down into the 8 to 9 foot zone where you feel them. Many fans ship with dual mounting, so they can be installed either way; check the box if you are not sure which you will need.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a middle ground worth knowing about. Some low-profile fans use a very short mount that leaves a slightly larger gap above the blades than a true hugger. On an 8-foot ceiling, those fans can squeeze out noticeably better airflow while still keeping the blades above 7 feet. Measure carefully; the spec sheet will list the overall fan height from ceiling to the bottom of the light kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Direction matters just as much for huggers as for downrod fans. A low-mounted fan in summer mode puts a strong breeze very close to the people below, which is great on hot nights, while winter mode in a low room rarely helps. Our guide to <a href="/ceiling-fan-direction-summer-and-winter/">ceiling fan direction in summer and winter</a> explains when to reverse and when to just switch the fan off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Outdoor Fans: Damp vs Wet Ratings and Sizing Outside
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoor ceiling fans follow the same sizing rules, with one change: size up. Outdoors there are no walls to contain the airflow, so a breeze dissipates faster and ambient wind competes with the fan. A covered patio of 200 square feet that would take a 44 to 52 inch fan indoors is better served by a 52 to 60 inch fan, and large covered porches often benefit from two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The UL rating matters more than the size. A damp-rated fan is built for covered locations that see humidity and condensation but never direct rain, such as a fully roofed and walled porch. A wet-rated fan is built for direct rain, wind-driven spray and sun, such as pergolas, gazebos and open patio covers. If water can ever reach the fan, buy wet-rated; a damp-rated fan in a wet location will corrode and fail early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearance rules still apply outdoors: blades at least 7 feet up, 18 inches from posts and walls, and a fan-rated box. Outdoor installs add one more: the box and wiring must be rated for the location, and the fan should be mounted under a solid roof section rather than a slatted pergola where possible, even if it is wet-rated, because constant direct exposure still shortens the life of the finish and light kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our pillar's outdoor pick is a wet-rated Minka-Aire model with sealed LED and weatherproof blades, and the full comparison of indoor and outdoor options is in our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">tested ceiling fans with lights</a>. Size it with the chart above, add a few inches for the outdoors, and match the rating to the worst weather the spot will see.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by the photo, not the room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fans look smaller hanging in a showroom with 20-foot ceilings than in a bedroom. Measure the floor area and use the chart. A 42-inch fan in a 300 square foot living room will run on high and still leave the corners still, while a well-sized 52-inch fan does the same job quietly on medium.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a hugger on a 9-foot ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flush-mount fans move noticeably less air because the blades sit too close to the ceiling to draw freely. They are the right call only when a downrod would drop the blades below 7 feet. On a 9-foot or taller ceiling, use at least the short included rod for better airflow at the same price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the 18-inch wall rule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oversized fan in a small room is the usual offender. Blade tips closer than 18 inches to a wall, beam or sloped ceiling create turbulence, noise and weaker airflow. Measure the room's narrowest dimension, subtract three feet for the clearances, and that is the largest blade span that will fit properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hanging on an old light box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A box that held a light fixture for twenty years is not automatically rated for a fan. Fans weigh more and vibrate constantly. Look for the fan-support marking and weight rating on the box, and if it is missing, replace it with a fan-rated box fixed to a joist or a brace bar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on blade span and ignoring CFM</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two fans with the same 52-inch span can differ by thousands of CFM. Check the EnergyGuide label for airflow on high and CFM per watt before you buy. A big fan with a weak motor and shallow blade pitch is the most common disappointment in this category, and it cannot be fixed afterward.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size ceiling fan do I need for a 12x12 room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12-by-12 room is 144 square feet, right at the top of the ENERGY STAR band for 36 to 42 inch fans. In practice a 42 to 44 inch fan is the best fit. A 52-inch fan also fits comfortably, leaving almost four feet to each wall, and is the better choice if you like a strong breeze on medium. Watch for door swings and tall furniture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size ceiling fan for a 12x14 or 14x16 bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12-by-14 bedroom is 168 square feet and suits a 44 to 52 inch fan; we would choose 52 inches and run it on medium. A 14-by-16 room is 224 square feet, which sits at the upper end of the same band, and a 52-inch fan is the standard choice. For bedrooms, prioritize a quiet DC motor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 52-inch ceiling fan too big for a bedroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for most bedrooms. A 52-inch fan suits rooms from roughly 150 to 350 square feet, which covers the typical primary and secondary bedroom. It is only physically too big when the room is under about 7.5 feet across, because the tips would end up closer than 18 inches to a wall, and it can feel overpowering in rooms under about 100 square feet. There, choose 36 to 44 inches.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How low can a ceiling fan hang?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The blades should be at least 7 feet above the floor, a minimum that virtually every manufacturer specifies in its installation instructions. The ideal height is 8 to 9 feet. On an 8-foot ceiling that means a hugger or low-profile fan; on a 7-foot-6 basement ceiling only the flattest huggers will keep the blades above the 7-foot line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What downrod length do I need for a 10-foot ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hunter recommends a 12 to 18 inch downrod for a 10-foot ceiling, which puts the blades at roughly 8 to 9 feet off the floor. The quick rule is ceiling height minus 9 feet. Check your fan's manual, because a tall motor housing or bowl light kit can change the answer by several inches.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many CFM should a ceiling fan have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a mid-size bedroom or living room, look for roughly 4,000 to 5,000 CFM on high. Large or open-plan rooms want 6,000 CFM or more. Small rooms and offices are fine with 2,500 to 3,500. The airflow figure is printed on the EnergyGuide label, along with wattage, so you can compare efficiency directly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need two ceiling fans for a large room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if the room is over about 400 square feet or more than one and a half times as long as it is wide. Split it into square zones and center a fan in each. Two well-placed 52-inch fans cover a long living room far better than one 70-inch fan in the middle, which leaves the ends still.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

